But wait, the "I have a todo" step calls `this.loadApp()` which creates a fresh window. And then "all existing todos are completed" doesn't call `this.loadApp()`. So the "I have a todo" creates a todo, then "all existing todos are completed" toggles all. This should work.

But actually, looking at the "I have a todo" step:
```javascript
Given('I have a todo {string}', function (text) {
  this.loadApp();
  const input = qs(this.document, '.new-todo');
  input.value = text;
  triggerKeyboardEvent(input, 'keydown', { key: 'Enter', keyCode: 13, which: 13 });
});
```

This calls `this.loadApp()` which creates a new window. Then it creates a todo "Buy milk". Then the "And all existing todos are completed" step doesn't reload, so it works with the same window. The "When I uncomplete" step also works with the same window. This should work.

But wait, there's also the "Given I have a completed todo" and "Given I am editing the todo" steps that call `this.loadApp()`. These are OK because they are the first step in the scenario (after the Before hook which already loaded the app).

Actually, looking at the "I have a todo" step - it's a Given step. If it's the first Given step in a scenario, the Before hook has already loaded the app. Then this step reloads it (creating a fresh window). This is fine - it's just a fresh start.

But what if a scenario has two Given steps like:
```
Given I have a todo "Buy milk"
And I complete the todo "Buy milk"
```

The first Given calls `this.loadApp()` and creates "Buy milk". The second Given (And) doesn't call `this.loadApp()` - it just toggles. This should work.

Now let me re-test.

